1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,700
Good morning, and welcome to Oniton Hall, one of the largest estates in the area.

2
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:09,960
My name's Nick, and I'm one of the guides.

3
00:00:09,960 --> 00:00:13,460
I'll give you a brief introduction to the estate

4
00:00:13,460 --> 00:00:16,850
while you're sitting down, and then we'll walk round.

5
00:00:16,850 --> 00:00:21,520
The estate consists of the house, gardens

6
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:27,020
parkland and farm, and it dates back to the fourteenth century.

7
00:00:27,020 --> 00:00:31,239
The original house was replaced in the late seventeenth century,

8
00:00:31,239 --> 00:00:35,880
and of course it has had a large number of owners.

9
00:00:35,880 --> 00:00:40,302
Almost all of them have left their mark,

10
00:00:40,302 --> 00:00:42,200
generally by adding new rooms

11
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:48,040
like the ballroom and conservatory, or by demolishing others.

12
00:00:48,040 --> 00:00:51,805
The farm looks much as it's always done,

13
00:00:51,805 --> 00:00:58,080
although the current owner has done a great deal of work to the flower beds.

14
00:00:58,080 --> 00:01:06,540
In the seventeenth century, the estate was owned by a very wealthy man called Sir Edward Downes.

15
00:01:06,540 --> 00:01:11,120
His intention was to escape from the world of politics

16
00:01:11,120 --> 00:01:14,430
after years as an active politician,

17
00:01:14,430 --> 00:01:22,320
and to build a new house worthy of his big collection of books, paintings and sculptures.

18
00:01:22,320 --> 00:01:27,146
He broke off contact with his former political allies,

19
00:01:27,146 --> 00:01:33,580
and hosted meetings of creative and literary people, like painters and poets.

20
00:01:33,580 --> 00:01:41,040
Unusually for his time, he didn't care whether his guests were rich or poor

21
00:01:41,040 --> 00:01:43,520
as long as they had talent.

22
00:01:43,520 --> 00:01:50,556
Big houses like Oniton had dozens of servants until the 1920s or 30s,

23
00:01:50,556 --> 00:01:57,050
and we've tried to show what their working lives were like.

24
00:01:57,050 --> 00:02:01,700
Photographs of course don't give much of an idea

25
00:02:02,180 --> 00:02:06,172
so instead, as you go round the house,

26
00:02:06,172 --> 00:02:12,760
you'll see volunteers dressed up as nineteenth-century servants, going about their work.

27
00:02:12,760 --> 00:02:15,640
They'll explain what they're doing

28
00:02:15,640 --> 00:02:21,130
and tell you their recipes, or what tools they're using.

29
00:02:21,130 --> 00:02:28,490
We've just introduced this feature to replace the audio guide we used to have available.

30
00:02:28,490 --> 00:02:32,970
I see there are a number of children here with you today.

31
00:02:32,970 --> 00:02:36,470
Well, we have several activities specially for children,

32
00:02:36,470 --> 00:02:42,596
like dressing up in the sorts of clothes that children wore in the past,

33
00:02:42,596 --> 00:02:46,400
and as it's a fine day

34
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:50,910
some of you will probably want to play in the adventure playground.

35
00:02:50,910 --> 00:03:01,000
Our latest addition is child-sized tractors, that you can drive around the grounds.

36
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:05,480
We'll also be going into the farm that's part of the estate

37
00:03:05,480 --> 00:03:07,870
where there's plenty to do.

38
00:03:07,870 --> 00:03:12,140
Most of the buildings date from the eighteenth century

39
00:03:12,260 --> 00:03:18,810
so you can really step back into an agricultural past.

40
00:03:18,810 --> 00:03:26,350
Until recently, the dairy was where milk from the cows was turned into cheese.

41
00:03:26,350 --> 00:03:31,100
It's now the place to go for lunch, or afternoon tea

42
00:03:31,100 --> 00:03:35,310
or just a cup of coffee and a slice of homemade cake.

43
00:03:35,310 --> 00:03:41,453
The big stone building that dominates the farm is the large barn,

44
00:03:41,453 --> 00:03:46,060
and in here is our collection of agricultural tools.

45
00:03:46,060 --> 00:03:49,860
These were used in the past to plough the earth

46
00:03:50,260 --> 00:03:53,770
sow seeds, make gates, and much more.

47
00:03:53,770 --> 00:03:55,913
There's a small barn,

48
00:03:55,913 --> 00:04:01,080
also made of stone, where you can groom the donkeys and horses

49
00:04:01,080 --> 00:04:03,200
to keep their coats clean.

50
00:04:03,200 --> 00:04:09,270
They really seem to enjoy having it done, and children love grooming them.

51
00:04:09,270 --> 00:04:12,900
The horses no longer live in the stables

52
00:04:13,260 --> 00:04:17,291
which instead is the place to go to buy gifts,

53
00:04:17,291 --> 00:04:20,520
books, our own jams and pickles

54
00:04:20,520 --> 00:04:24,420
and clothes and blankets made of wool from our sheep.

55
00:04:24,420 --> 00:04:28,657
Outside the shed, which is the only brick building,

56
00:04:28,657 --> 00:04:33,820
you can climb into a horse-drawn carriage for a lovely

57
00:04:33,820 --> 00:04:36,660
relaxing tour of the park and farm.

58
00:04:36,660 --> 00:04:40,920
The carriages are well over a hundred years old.

59
00:04:40,920 --> 00:04:47,035
And finally, the parkland, which was laid out in the eighteenth century,

60
00:04:47,035 --> 00:04:52,130
with a lake and trees that are now well established.

61
00:04:52,130 --> 00:04:58,010
You'll see types of cattle and sheep that are hardly ever found on farms these days.

62
00:04:58,010 --> 00:05:03,230
We're helping to preserve them, to stop their numbers falling further.

63
00:05:03,230 --> 00:05:09,000
OK, well if you'd like to come with me...
